# Snapped Phish-ing Line
Room Overview
As a member of the IT department at SwiftSpend Financial, multiple employees across departments report receiving a suspicious email. Several have already submitted credentials and lost account access. The incident is escalated for full investigation — identify the attacker's infrastructure, recover the phishing kit, and determine the scope of compromise.
Category: Phishing Investigation / CTI
Objective

Identify key artifacts from provided email samples
Investigate phishing URLs and redirection chain
Retrieve and analyze the phishing kit
Gather adversary intelligence via CTI tools
Uncover additional indicators within the phishing kit

Tools & Environment

TryHackMe AttackBox (isolated VM — safe to interact with live URLs and attachments)
VirusTotal — file hash reputation and threat categorization
CyberChef — decoding encoded strings
CLI — sha256sum, unzip, find

Investigation Process
Starting with the phish-emails folder — 5 emails to review. Opening Quote for Services Rendered first, examining headers to extract recipient name and adversary sender address. Headers are the first stop before touching any attachments or links.
Moving to the email addressed to Zoe Duncan — contains an attachment. Since we're operating in an isolated VM, it's safe to open and observe where it leads. The attachment redirects to a Microsoft login impersonation page on the domain kennaroads[.]buzz.
With the phishing domain identified, checking for exposed files on the attacker's server — navigating to /data/ reveals an open directory containing update365.zip. This is a common OPSEC failure on the attacker's side and worth checking early in any phishing investigation. The archive is downloaded and hashed:
sha256sum update365.zip
ba3c15267393419eb08c7b2652b8b6b39b406ef300ae8a18fee4d16b19ac9686
Hash is run through VirusTotal — confirms phishing classification and reveals an additional threat category assigned to the archive, along with a file count from the Details tab.
Navigating to /data/Update365/ — a log.txt file is present containing submitted credentials. The log is short enough to manually identify a user who submitted credentials more than once without tooling.
Extracting the archive and locating submit.php to identify the exfiltration email address:
find -type f -name submit.php 2>/dev/null
Final step — locating the flag. Directory traversal: /data/flag.txt → not there. /data/Update365/ → not there. Found at /data/Update365/office365/flag.txt. Value is Base64 encoded, decoded in reverse via CyberChef to retrieve the flag.
Findings

Attack chain: Phishing email → malicious attachment → redirect → Microsoft credential harvesting page → exfiltration via submit.php
Phishing domain: kennaroads[.]buzz
Phishing kit hash: ba3c15267393419eb08c7b2652b8b6b39b406ef300ae8a18fee4d16b19ac9686
Exposed credential log: /data/Update365/log.txt
Exfiltration method: Credentials collected via submit.php and forwarded to adversary-controlled email

Analysis & Response

MITRE ATT&CK: T1566 (Phishing), T1078 (Valid Accounts — harvested credentials)
Immediate actions: Reset credentials for all affected accounts, prioritizing users identified in log.txt. Block kennaroads[.]buzz at the perimeter.
Communication: Notify all recipients of the phishing campaign regardless of whether they submitted credentials
Longer term: Phishing awareness training; enforce MFA to limit impact of credential harvesting

Key Takeaways

Phishing kits frequently expose themselves via open /data/ directories — directory enumeration is a high-value early step in phishing investigations
Email headers are the primary artifact for sender attribution before any attachment interaction
Environment matters: safe interaction with live URLs and attachments is only viable in a properly isolated VM
CyberChef handles most encoding/decoding quickly during triage — keep it in the toolkit
